Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant tables / columns and their potential usage

veg_loss_rate_df  
 • "item_code": join key to wholesale list  
 • "item_name": readable description of the SKU  
 • "loss_rate_%": numeric loss‑rate we need to analyse  

veg_whsle_df  
 • "item_code": list of the wholesale SKUs we are interested in.  
We will only analyse loss rates for items that actually occur in this wholesale file
           (WHERE item_code IN (SELECT DISTINCT item_code FROM veg_whsle_df)).

Conditions we will repeatedly use  
 •  item_code IN (SELECT DISTINCT item_code FROM veg_whsle_df)  – only keep wholesale SKUs  
 •  Loss‑rate summary thresholds will be built from AVG() and a population standard deviation  
    stdev = sqrt( avg(rate²) – avg(rate)² )  

Below are ten exploratory‑to‑final SQL queries (each limited to 20 rows where practical).  
All identifiers that contain blanks, parentheses or % are wrapped in double quotes.

```sql
-- 1. Show a sample of wholesale item codes
SELECT DISTINCT "item_code"
FROM "veg_whsle_df"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. What loss‑rate records do we have for those wholesale items?
SELECT DISTINCT wl."item_code",
       lr."item_name",
       lr."loss_rate_%"
FROM   "veg_whsle_df" AS wl
JOIN   "veg_loss_rate_df" AS lr
       USING ("item_code")
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Inspect the numeric spread of loss rates
SELECT DISTINCT lr."loss_rate_%"
FROM   "veg_loss_rate_df" AS lr
WHERE  lr."item_code" IN (SELECT DISTINCT "item_code" FROM "veg_whsle_df")
ORDER BY lr."loss_rate_%"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Compute the average loss rate across wholesale SKUs
SELECT AVG(lr."loss_rate_%") AS avg_loss_rate
FROM   "veg_loss_rate_df" AS lr
WHERE  lr."item_code" IN (SELECT DISTINCT "item_code" FROM "veg_whsle_df");
```

```sql
-- 5. Compute avg of squared values – helper for population stdev
SELECT AVG( (lr."loss_rate_%" * lr."loss_rate_%") ) AS avg_square
FROM   "veg_loss_rate_df" AS lr
WHERE  lr."item_code" IN (SELECT DISTINCT "item_code" FROM "veg_whsle_df");
```

```sql
-- 6. Bring average and stdev together in one CTE
WITH metrics AS (
  SELECT AVG(lr."loss_rate_%")                                            AS avg_rate,
         sqrt( AVG(lr."loss_rate_%"*lr."loss_rate_%") -
               AVG(lr."loss_rate_%")*AVG(lr."loss_rate_%") )              AS sd_rate
  FROM   "veg_loss_rate_df" AS lr
  WHERE  lr."item_code" IN (SELECT DISTINCT "item_code" FROM "veg_whsle_df")
)
SELECT *
FROM   metrics;
